    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-7",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-7",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Throat sensation and associated symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Reports intermittent throat tightness, especially in the evenings when lying down, and a sensation like something is stuck; described as recurring. No true pain, dysphagia to solids or fluids, odynophagia, choking, or regurgitation. Mild intermittent hoarseness, especially after a full day of teaching or yelling. No coryzal symptoms, cough, fever, weight loss, or night sweats."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-8",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-8",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Premenstrual symptoms and pattern"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Reports longstanding premenstrual irritability, marked mood swings, bloating, breast tenderness, and headaches in the week before menses; symptoms settle 1–2 days after bleeding starts, and she is essentially well mid-cycle. Cycles regular, every 28–30 days; bleeding lasts 5–6 days. A symptom diary was only partly completed for one cycle; the recorded pattern showed luteal-phase symptoms resolving after menses. Physical premenstrual symptoms are milder than mood symptoms and irritability. Reports increased conflict at work and with partner during the premenstrual week."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-9",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-9",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Mood and safety symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Reports no persistent low mood or anhedonia outside the premenstrual week. Denies suicidal ideation and self-harm. No psychosis reported. Mentions being somewhat more short-fused through the month since starting with a new class; this was not explored further at this visit."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-10",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-10",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Stress and concern about throat symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Reports increased stress this term and thinks chalk dust may be contributing. Very anxious about possible thyroid disease or cancer after searching online."
      }
    },
    {
